# --- observability help module ------------------------------------------------
_devhelp_observability() {
cat <<'EOF2'
observability — self-hosted SigNoz on Colima (local only)
=========================================================

  obs_start      start (forges first if needed); waits for the UI
  obs_status     containers, UI and OTLP health, first-run hints
  obs_stop       stop, data kept        obs_down   remove containers, data kept
  obs_logs [s]   follow logs (e.g. obs_logs signoz-signoz-0)
  obs_forge      regenerate the compose files from the casting (after edits/upgrades)

  UI    http://localhost:18080   (port: the patch at the end of the casting)
  OTLP  gRPC localhost:4317 · HTTP localhost:4318   (what apps send telemetry to)

First run: create the admin account in the UI. SigNoz only configures its
collector once an organization exists; until then 4317/4318 don't answer.

What runs (6 containers, ~0.6 GB RAM idle)
  ingester          SigNoz's OpenTelemetry collector: receives OTLP, writes ClickHouse
  signoz            API + UI; also configures the collector over OpAMP (port 4320)
  clickhouse        column store for traces, metrics, logs (docker volume)
  clickhouse-keeper coordination for ClickHouse (like ZooKeeper)
  postgres          SigNoz's own metadata: users, dashboards, alerts
  migrator          one-shot schema migrations, exits when done

Files
  casting   ~/.config/signoz/casting.yaml  (repo: DotFiles/config/signoz/)
            every image pinned by digest; stats reporter + analytics OFF
  compose   ~/.local/share/signoz/pours/   generated by foundryctl; don't edit, re-forge
  data      docker volumes signoz-*        survive obs_stop/obs_down

Upgrade: in the casting, change tag AND digest of an image
  docker pull <image>:<tag>
  docker image inspect <image>:<tag> --format '{{index .RepoDigests 0}}'
then obs_forge && obs_start. Never use :latest (the collector's latest was an rc).

Learning: the three signals
  traces   one request as a tree of timed spans → flame graph / Gantt view in
           the trace explorer; find the slow step, not just the slow request
  metrics  numbers over time (counters, gauges, histograms) → dashboards, alerts;
           think rate(), p50/p95/p99, per-label breakdowns (model, tool, config)
  logs     events with attributes → search, and jump from a span to its logs
  RED      Rate, Errors, Duration per operation: the first dashboard for anything
  USE      Utilization, Saturation, Errors per resource (CPU, memory, disk)

Poke at it: send one span by hand
  curl --noproxy '*' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
    http://localhost:4318/v1/traces -d '{"resourceSpans":[{"resource":{"attributes":
    [{"key":"service.name","value":{"stringValue":"hello"}}]},"scopeSpans":[{"spans":
    [{"traceId":"5b8efff798038103d269b633813fc60c","spanId":"eee19b7ec3c1b174",
    "name":"hi","kind":1,"startTimeUnixNano":"1","endTimeUnixNano":"2"}]}]}]}'
  ClickHouse directly (SQL on your telemetry):
    docker exec -it signoz-telemetrystore-clickhouse-0-0 clickhouse-client
    SHOW TABLES FROM signoz_traces;   SHOW TABLES FROM signoz_metrics;
EOF2
}

_devhelp_register "observability" _devhelp_observability
